# 14. Side-chat concept

A session may contain a primary reasoning thread and temporary branches.

Example:

```text
Main Session
    │
    ├── message
    ├── message
    │
    ├── Side Chat
    │      ├── question
    │      └── explanation
    │
    └── continue main context
```

A side-chat:

- inherits selected context from its parent;
- does not automatically contaminate the main conversation;
- may optionally promote a result back into the parent session.

This concept belongs to session/application architecture, not Provider Gateway.

Further explanation:
# Side Chat / Ephemeral Branches

## Product concept: Side Chat

Side Chat allows a user to temporarily branch from the current
conversation to ask a secondary question without contaminating or
redirecting the main conversation context.

Example:

Main conversation:
A → B → C ─────────────→ D
          │
          └→ Side Chat
              Q → A

After closing the Side Chat, the main conversation continues from C.

Typical use cases:

- ask what a concept means;
- clarify something the model just said;
- explore an alternative without changing the main reasoning thread;
- ask a temporary implementation/detail question.

## Architecture concept: Ephemeral Branch

A Side Chat is implemented as an Ephemeral Branch of a conversation/session.

An Ephemeral Branch:

- has a parent conversation/thread;
- branches from a specific point in that thread;
- inherits the relevant parent context up to that point;
- maintains its own messages;
- does not automatically add its messages to the parent context;
- can be discarded without affecting the parent;
- may eventually allow selected information to be promoted back to
  the parent conversation.

This distinction implies an important architectural principle:

Conversation history != Model context.

The session may know that branches existed, while the Context Builder
determines which messages are included in each model invocation.

